Notes for Testing
• Set header Content-Type: application/json for every POST and PUT.
• Run request 1 first to observe the raw 400 response with no field-level detail, then request 2 with fully valid
data to get a 201 and capture the generated customerId for use as {id} in the remaining requests.
• Request 4 reuses that same id but sends a malformed email in the PUT body to confirm @Valid is applied
identically on update, not just on create.
• Save all five requests into the Postman Collection for reuse in the next practical, where a
GlobalExceptionHandler will turn these bare 400 responses into structured per-field error messages.
How to Run
1. Place the files in the package structure shown above.
2. Run mvn spring-boot:run (or run CustomerValidationApiApplication from an IDE).
3. The API is available at http://localhost:8080/api/customers.
4. Open http://localhost:8080/h2-console and connect with JDBC URL
jdbc:h2:mem:customervaldb, username sa, empty password.
5. Execute the Postman requests in order: invalid POST (400) → valid POST (201) → GET by id (200) →
invalid PUT (400) → DELETE (204).
